{"kind":"report","text":"R15 rulings (answers 726ed54e).\n\n1. Revoker carrier: accepted. The retire control body carries one exact signed revoke-key intent (as: retired) by a key that membership admits for it (membership.manage or recovery). Its actor can differ from the controller. The endpoint persists that envelope unchanged and submits it. A retry submits only the same envelope.\n2. Seed first. Retire erases the agent's seed at its start, in the same storage transaction that marks the row retiring. The revoke is signed by another key, so the seed is never needed again. The agent can then sign nothing, even if the revoke is late.\n3. Never enrolled. A key that was never admitted needs no revoke. If its invitation is still open, the retire carries the manager's signed cancellation, or waits for the invitation's end. The outcome is `cancelled-before-enrollment`, with the seed erased.\n4. Legacy retired rows: an admitted repair is accepted. An admin control applies the new retire to a row in state retired that still holds a seed or whose key is still active in membership: seed erased, then the carried revoke.\n5. Permanent failure. If the carried revoke is refused or expires before acceptance, the row stays `retiring`, with the seed already erased. It completes by either of two things: a new manager-signed revoke in a new control operation (a new op, never a retry of the old one), or proof read from membership that the key is already revoked by any accepted act. The answer names which. A retire is never reported complete without one of them.\n6. No historical cohort changes. Witnesses: add one for each of items 2 to 5.","body":{"to":"builder"}}